    (0, None, '\'Listen, Oguzok, come here to me.\' "Slushay" is the everyday \'listen\' or \'hey\' you put before a request. "Podoydi ko mne", \'come over to me\', is the chef\'s call from the cod meeting again.'),
    (1, None, '\'Yes, chef! What needs to be done?\' "Nuzhno", \'it\'s needed\', names nobody. "Sdelat" is \'to do\', in the form for getting a job done.'),
    (2, None, '\'Go to the bar and bring cognac.\' "Skhodi" adds "s-" to "khodi" and means \'pop over there and back\'. "Prinesi", \'bring\', begins with "pri-", which points toward the speaker.'),
    (3, None, '\'Which cognac exactly do you need?\' Literally \'which exactly cognac to you is needed\'. "Nuzhen" keeps its bare form to match "konyak", and "vam" is the polite \'to you\'.'),
