    (5, None, '\'And what if someone notices me?\' A "esli" is \'and if\', the way to start a worried \'what if\'. "Zametit", \'will notice\', is a single future moment.'),
    (6, None, '\'Go to the bar to keep up appearances.\' "Dlya otvoda glaz" is a fixed phrase, literally \'for the leading away of eyes\': a decoy. Learn it as one chunk.'),
    (7, None, '\'And what should I say to bartender Kostya?\' "Chto skazat" is \'what to say\', no \'should\' needed. Both "barmen" and Kostya change because they\'re \'to\' someone: "barmenu Koste".'),
    (8, None, '\'Say that this is for meat sauce.\' "Myaso", \'meat\', becomes an adjective here: "myasnoy", \'meaty\'. After "dlya" it ends up as "myasnogo sousa".'),
    (9, None, '\'Understood, I will do everything quickly and quietly.\' "Ponyal", \'got it\', is all you need in a kitchen. Like most \'how\' words, "bystro" and "tikho" finish with -o.'),
    (10, None, '\'And stuff the fish with julienne and lemon.\' "Farshiruy" comes from "farsh", \'stuffing\' or \'mince\'. "Zhyulenom" with no preposition is \'with julienne\' as the filling, while "s limonom" is \'together with lemon\'.'),
